{
  "version": "2.0.0",
  "description": "Stack detection and knowledge binding. The orchestrator detects the stack from marker files and source extensions, then binds the language packs, test runner, coverage command and automation frameworks WITHOUT asking the user. Role agents (code-reviewer, build-resolver, test-author, ...) are bound per phase and specialise by loading the packs a stack names - onestop has one reviewer that knows many languages, not one agent per language. Ask only when detection returns two or more stacks of equal confidence in the same component directory.",
  "detection": {
    "order": [
      "1. Read onestop.yml at the repository root if present - committed and authoritative. Its stack.components replace detection for the paths they declare; its commands replace resolution for the commands they name.",
      "2. Read .github/tech-stack.md then .claude/tech-stack.md - authoritative if present.",
      "3. Match strong marker files. A marker several stacks claim (build.gradle.kts for Java and Kotlin) says which family, not which stack, and its score is shared between them.",
      "4. Weak markers count only alongside an extension census above 30 percent, and a framework stack binds only when one of its dependency_markers is in the manifest.",
      "5. Fall back to the source file extension census.",
      "6. Still unknown: ask ONE question naming the top candidates found, never an open-ended question."
    ],
    "confidence_rule": "A strong marker scores 1.0 divided by the number of stacks that claim it, a dependency marker 1.0, a weak marker 0.3 (only with an extension census above 30 percent), an extension census above 60 percent 0.7 and below that 0.3. A stack binds at 0.7 or more; a framework stack binds only on a dependency hit. Two stacks within 0.2 of each other in the SAME component that share no pack are a genuine ambiguity - ask. Different directories means multi-component - bind both.",
    "commands": "Command defaults below are used only when onestop.yml, CI and the task runner say nothing, and only when everything in requires is present in the manifest (a nested list means any one of them). {x} is the package runner for the lockfile (npx, pnpm exec, yarn, bunx); {py} is \"uv run \" or \"poetry run \" when that lockfile exists. A default whose requirements are missing is offered to the user as a candidate, never run."
  },
